\section{Consing, Roots, and Reachability}

A \texttt{cons} produces a pair with two fields and can reuse an
existing tail. The resulting heap is a graph, not a collection
of independent list-shaped allocations. Winston and Horn's
\textit{LISP} explains this through networks of cells, free
storage, and destructive operations that change existing links.
After destructive updates, an unreachable cycle can contain
objects with references to one another. Collecting only objects
with a reference count of zero cannot reclaim that cycle by itself.
Nicholas Pippenger's \textit{Pure versus Impure Lisp} makes
pair mutation the dividing line in a comparison of abstract
Lisp machines. Constructing a new pair that points to existing
pairs can introduce sharing without a cycle; redirecting an
existing pair's field to itself creates a cycle without
allocating anything. The tracing algorithm must handle both.

A tracing collector starts from roots: for example, live registers
and stack slots, global bindings, and handles registered by foreign
code. It follows the pointer fields of visited objects. The safety
invariant is that everything reachable through strong references
from a root survives, with its identity relationships unchanged.
Whether a particular root set includes all interned symbols or
whether a weak reference keeps its referent alive depends on the
runtime's rules. Tracing is not a prediction of what the program
will read in the future; a program that retains an unused table in
a global binding can still cause a memory leak.

\section{Moving Objects and Choosing What to Scan}

A mark-and-sweep collector marks reachable cells and then walks
storage to reclaim unmarked cells. A copying collector can instead
put survivors together in new storage, leaving dead objects behind.
In Cheney's semispace algorithm, copied but unscanned objects form
the queue between a scan cursor and the allocation cursor in the
destination space. Forwarding records in the source space ensure
that the second edge to a copied object is rewritten to the same
destination as the first. Once the scan cursor catches the
allocation cursor, all destination objects have been scanned and
the old space can be reused. This description assumes the program
is stopped and that the runtime can distinguish pointer fields
from immediate values. Andrew W. Appel's \textit{A Runtime System}
uses Standard ML of New Jersey to demonstrate the same design
concern outside Lisp: the layout of values and records matters
to both the code generator and the collector.

David A. Moon's \textit{Garbage Collection in a Large Lisp System}
describes adding incremental copying and ephemeral-object collection
to the Symbolics 3600. Incremental work spreads collection across
program execution and must enforce rules about accessing objects that
have not yet been evacuated. Generational work avoids repeatedly
tracing long-lived regions when collecting short-lived objects. The
two ideas solve different problems. Moon also explains an
approximately depth-first scan that prioritizes the partially filled
destination page, intending to keep related objects physically close
in a demand-paged memory. That choice trades extra scanning for
better expected locality; Moon reports a scanning cost comparison
but does not claim to have measured the resulting locality gain.

For instance, an incremental copying collector can use a read
barrier to ensure that when program code fetches a pointer into
the evacuated region, it obtains the forwarded object, moving
the object if necessary. A different barrier strategy would need
its own invariant. It is not safe just to run an ordinary
stop-the-world scan in small pieces while allowing arbitrary
heap reads and writes between them.

\section{Worked Test: An Old Object Points to a Young One}

Start with a rooted old pair \(P\). Allocate a young pair \(Y\),
store it in both fields of \(P\), then drop every other reference
to \(Y\). Trigger a young-only collection. If the collector does
not scan the old generation, its roots for the young collection
must include incoming pointers from the old generation. A write
barrier can record the changed object or dirty its containing card
so the minor collector visits that region. Otherwise the collector
sees no path to \(Y\) and may reclaim it even though mutator code
can still find it.

After collection, read both fields of \(P\) and check that they refer
to the same pair and its contents are intact. If \(Y\) moved, both
fields must be updated; preserving just its contents twice is not
enough. Add an unreferenced cycle of young pairs and verify, using
collector instrumentation, that the cycle does not survive. The
same distinction between tracked dependencies and abandoned cycles
applies to compiled artifacts in Chapter~21 of \textit{The Modern
Compiler Dossier}. For the costs exposed by these collector choices,
see Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-158}; for general tracing mechanisms, see
Chapter~\ref{ch:garbage-collection}.
